>=20
> >
> > This would mean that to improve the issues reported in [2],=20
> > async_schedule() has to be implemented for the sub systems used,=20
> > there, too? I.e. the USB init?
>=20
> USB init is asynchronous internal to USB; this was one of the outcomes=20
> of a side discussion at the Kernel Summit a year and a half ago or so=20
> where Alan Stern visualized the problem... and solved it internal to USB.
>=20
> Now, there is one 100 msec delay that is inherent to USB (mandatory=20
> delay required by the spec for voltage to stabilize) but that shouldn't=
=20
> be in the synchronous path anymore

There is a 100ms electrical/mechanical root debounce delay from USB=20
specification that is indeed avoided by [1]. What we see is that=20
there are two (EHCI + OHCI) additional 50ms root port reset delays=20
required by USB spec resulting in an 100ms delay. Seems that these=20
two are synchronous(?) See bootgraph pictures attached.

> >>> what kernel are you seeing issues on?
> >>
> >> [1] talks about 2.6.28, [2] talks about 2.6.34.
>=20
> yeah 2.6.28 is a lost cause ;-)  2.6.34 should be not too bad.
>=20
> Now, the one thing that's critical for all of this is that we need to do=
=20
> this data driven. Using async init is not always as easy as it sounds;=20
> there are device ordering things that need to be dealt with, and failure=
=20
> cases get much more complex as well.
>=20
> So the first order of business ALWAYS is to get a bootgraph (from=20
> scripts/bootgraph.pl)... to see which things are big on the one hand,=20
> but also to see if there is gains to be had. There is absolutely no gain=
=20
> possible (in terms of making something async) if it is pretty much the=20
> last big thing in the boot that you are making asynchronous (since the=20
> end of kernel boot has to wait for it all anyway); there is only gains=20
> for async if the expensive operation, when made async, can run in=20
> parallel with something else expensive that comes later in the kernel ini=
t.
>=20
> The bootgraph will show this opportunity / pitfall very clearly......

Please find in the attachment two bootgraphs from the 2.6.34 system=20
discussed in [2].

The first is from the unmodified boot. The second with having=20
"fastboot: turn the USB hostcontroller initcalls into async=20
initcalls" applied.

Does this make sense?
